  "slug": "miscanthus-genomic-prediction-9b6c",
  "summary": "Sparse testing designs and multi-environment genomic models are being benchmarked to reduce phenotyping burden in Miscanthus breeding.",
  "synthesis": "The three preprints converge on genomic prediction for Miscanthus breeding populations evaluated across multiple environments. Their common thread is explicit modeling of genotype-by-environment structure rather than reliance on main-effect models. One study reports that GxE-aware models raise predictive ability over main-effect models across cross-validation schemes for biomass yield. A second evaluates sparse testing designs for multi-environment trials, finding that the GxE model performs best and that phenotyping costs could be cut about fivefold. A third benchmarks five genomic prediction models, including multi-trait multi-environment approaches, on 336 M. sacchariflorus genotypes under three cross-validation schemes. What is new is the joint focus on testing-design economy and model structure in this crop.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-03-23"
}
